window.GAKS_SEARCH = [{"t":"The SUT model","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-001-01-the-sut-model","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-001-01-the-sut-model.html","s":"draft","x":"The SUT model Definition One JSON document that declares a system under test: its states (places a journey can be, each with a marker that proves arrival), its elements (named locators), its actions (steps the SUT offers — the edges of the graph), its journeys (named paths through the actions) and its endpoints (the API surface). The contract is SPEC-taf-v1-001-04. Why it exists A test suite is usually a pile of files that encode knowledge of the SUT invisibly. Here the knowledge is written down once, as data: the same model generates the Python suite and the Java suite, refuses broken input with named reasons, and later is the graph that model-based walks run on. Change the model, regenerate, and the suite's diff is the change — visible in a pull request. In this repo sut.model.json per SUT (none yet — lane A-1 builds the loader). Read SPEC-taf-v1-001-04 for the clauses and the refusals."},{"t":"The seeded walk","i":"CONCEPT-dsl-v1-002-01-the-seeded-walk","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-dsl-v1-002-01-the-seeded-walk.html","s":"draft","x":"The seeded walk Definition A random test is only useful if it can fail twice. A seeded walk drives a pseudo-random number generator with a recorded seed, so the \"random\" journey it derived is exactly reproducible: same model, same start, same seed — the same steps. The walk's output is a journey declaration in the model's own JSON shape, so the replay is data you can commit, review, and emit — not a log file only a runner understands. python -m taf walk model.json --from board --seed 42 --steps 6 walk.json python -m taf emit --lang python model.json -o suite --journeys walk.json Why it exists MBT without replay is a bug report that says \"it failed once.\" Seed replay turns exploration into a regression asset: commit the seed (or the derived journey), and the failure is a named test forever. --cover prefers untaken edges — the first step toward coverage-directed generation. In this repo taf/graph.py — edges derives adjacency from declared from/to; walk traverses it under random.Random(seed); withjourneys merges derived journeys into a copy of the model. T-taf-006 proves determinism, coverage, and the merge path."},{"t":"The JUnit lifecycle","i":"CONCEPT-java-v1-001-01-the-junit-lifecycle","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-java-v1-001-01-the-junit-lifecycle.html","s":"draft","x":"The JUnit lifecycle Definition JUnit 5's fixture mechanism: annotated methods JUnit runs around tests. @BeforeAll/@AfterAll run once per class (static, expensive setup like launching a browser); @BeforeEach/@AfterEach bracket every @Test (fresh context, fresh page). It is the same idea as the pytest fixture — setup and guaranteed teardown — expressed as annotations on a base class. java @BeforeAll static void launch() { browser = playwright.chromium().launch(); } @BeforeEach void fresh() { context = browser.newContext(); page = context.newPage(); } @AfterEach void closeContext() { context.close(); } @AfterAll static void shutdown() { browser.close(); } Why it exists Generated Java tests get the same isolation contract as the pytest ones (session browser, per-test context) through a different mechanism — proof that the model's semantics survive the language change and only the idiom moves. In this repo generated-java//src/test/java/taf/generated/BaseTest.java — every generated JUnit suite inherits its lifecycle from this class."},{"t":"The locator ladder","i":"CONCEPT-playwright-v1-001-01-the-locator-ladder","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-playwright-v1-001-01-the-locator-ladder.html","s":"draft","x":"The locator ladder Definition The ordered preference for how a test finds a UI element: 1. role + name — what the element is to a user (button \"Save\") 2. label / text / placeholder — what it says 3. testid (data-testid) — a deliberate marker the SUT provides 4. css / xpath — how it happens to be built; last resort, flagged fragile Why it exists Tests that find elements by structure break when structure changes; tests that find them by meaning break only when meaning changes. The ladder is Playwright's own guidance made mechanical: in sut.model.json a locator declares one of the rungs and :bad-locator refuses anything else, so the preference is enforced by the model, not by habit. In this repo SPEC-taf-v1-001-04, elements clause. The emitters translate each rung to the platform's locator API (getbyrole → getByRole)."},{"t":"The browser context","i":"CONCEPT-playwright-v1-002-01-the-browser-context","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-playwright-v1-002-01-the-browser-context.html","s":"draft","x":"The browser context Definition Playwright's unit of isolation: a browser.newcontext() is an independent session — own cookies, storage, cache — inside one browser. Each test gets a fresh context, so one test's login or localStorage can never leak into the next. Why it exists The honest alternative is a fresh browser per test, which is slow, or a shared browser, which leaks state and makes failures order-dependent. A context is fast and isolated — test independence by construction, which is what makes generated suites safe to run in any order or in parallel later. In this repo generated//conftest.py — the page fixture creates and closes a context around every test."},{"t":"Auto-waiting assertions","i":"CONCEPT-playwright-v1-003-01-auto-waiting","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-playwright-v1-003-01-auto-waiting.html","s":"draft","x":"Auto-waiting assertions Definition expect(locator).tobevisible() does not check once — it retries until the page catches up or the timeout fails the test. Playwright's locators and assertions wait for the condition instead of racing the browser. Why it exists The classic cause of flaky tests is the race between the test and the app: click() before the button renders, assert before the page settles. Explicit sleep() calls are how suites rot. Auto-waiting makes the honest default also the fast one — the test proceeds the moment the SUT is actually ready. In this repo generated//pages.py — expectarrived() is an auto-waiting marker check; every state transition in a journey waits on the SUT's proof, never on a timer."},{"t":"The page object","i":"CONCEPT-pom-v1-001-01-the-page-object","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-pom-v1-001-01-the-page-object.html","s":"draft","x":"The page object Definition A class that is a page or state of the SUT: it holds the page's locators and its meaningful operations, so tests say board.expectarrived() instead of repeating selectors. python class BoardPage: def init(self, page): self.marker = page.locator(\".ikc-dock-theme\") def expectarrived(self): expect(self.marker).tobevisible() Why it exists Locators are the most-fragile line in a suite. When they live in one class per state, a UI change is a one-place fix — and tests read as intent (\"arrive at the board\"), not mechanics. In tafbase the pattern is stricter: a page object's marker is the proof of arrival the model declares, so the class also knows when its page is real. In this repo generated//pages.py — one class per declared state, generated from states in the model (SPEC-taf-v1-001-04)."},{"t":"The pytest fixture","i":"CONCEPT-pytest-v1-001-01-the-pytest-fixture","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-pytest-v1-001-01-the-pytest-fixture.html","s":"draft","x":"The pytest fixture Definition A function pytest calls to provide something a test needs — a browser, a page, a base URL — and to clean it up after. yield splits the two halves: everything before it is setup, everything after is teardown that runs even when the test fails. python @pytest.fixture def page(browser): pg = browser.newpage() setup yield pg handed to the test pg.close() teardown, always scope= says how often the fixture is made: function (every test — the default), class, module, session (once for the whole run). Why it exists Tests should not repeat their plumbing. Fixtures give each test a fresh, named dependency — and the scope gives control over cost: a browser is expensive (session), a clean page is cheap (function). In this repo generated//conftest.py — the emitted suites get baseurl, browser and page fixtures for free."},{"t":"Environment variables","i":"CONCEPT-python-v1-001-01-the-environment","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-python-v1-001-01-the-environment.html","s":"draft","x":"Environment variables Definition Every process inherits a set of text key=value pairs from the program that started it — its environment. The shell exports them; the program reads them. In Python they live in os.environ, a dict-like mapping: python os.environ.get(\"TAFBASEURL\", \"http://127.0.0.1:4856\") value, or a default os.environ.get(\"TAFIKC3WS\") value, or None Java reads the same table through System.getenv() — one mechanism, two spellings, which is why both generated suites retarget the same way. A variable set for one command applies to that run only: bash TAFSLOWMO=800 .venv/bin/python -m pytest generated/python/ikc3-tracker -q (Not this word's other meaning: the virtual environment is a different thing — CONCEPT-python-v1-002-01-the-virtual-environment.) Why it exists Generated code is immutable — you edit the model and regenerate, never the output. But where the SUT runs changes run to run. The environment is the seam where run-to-run variation belongs: the same committed suite tests a probe bus, a staging box, or production by setting a variable, not by editing code. The previous-generation framework externalized the same seam into config.properties files; environment variables are the twelve-factor form of the same idea. In this project variable read by meaning ---------------- ----------------------------- --------------------------------------------- TAFBASEURL conftest.py, BaseTest where the SUT lives — probe, staging, prod TAFIKC3WS pytestruntestmakereport when set, a red test files a fault report TAFHEADLESS conftest.py, BaseTest 1 for CI; headed is the default TAFSLOWMO conftest.py, BaseTest ms between Playwright actions TAFLIVE tests/testlive.py opts the live acceptance tests in TAFBASEURL appears in taf/emitpython.py, taf/emitjava.py, and every emitted conftest.py/BaseTest.java. TAFIKC3WS drives CONCEPT-taf-v1-002-01-the-result-sink. Touch it bash env grep TAF what is set right now — usually nothing TAFBASEURL=http://x .venv/bin/python -m taf check suts/ikc3-tracker.sut.model.json Neighbors The virtual environment (above); the result sink the TAFIKC3WS variable enables; CONCEPT-taf-v1-001-01-the-learning-path."},{"t":"The virtual environment","i":"CONCEPT-python-v1-002-01-the-virtual-environment","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-python-v1-002-01-the-virtual-environment.html","s":"draft","x":"The virtual environment Definition A virtual environment — .venv/ in this repo — is a directory that redirects where Python looks for packages. It is not a copy of Python. Inside: .venv/ ├── bin/python a stub — the same interpreter, different eyes ├── bin/pip installs into THIS directory ├── pyvenv.cfg home = /usr/bin — where the real interpreter lives └── lib/python3.12/site-packages/ this project's own package store Running .venv/bin/python is the mechanism: the interpreter reads pyvenv.cfg beside itself, learns include-system-site-packages = false, and resolves imports in .venv/lib/.../site-packages only. \"Activating\" (source .venv/bin/activate) is merely a convenience that puts .venv/bin first on PATH so a bare python resolves there — this repo never relies on it and always names .venv/bin/python explicitly. The path is the activation. Why it exists Packages collide. pip install playwright without a venv writes into one shared site-packages for the whole system: a second project needing another version, or an OS tool written in Python, now fights for the same slots. A venv gives each project its own package store, so pytest 9 here and pytest 7 elsewhere coexist, and so python3 -m venv .venv && .venv/bin/pip install pytest playwright reproduces this toolchain on any machine without touching — or needing permission to touch — the system Python. In this project .venv/ is gitignored — it is recreated, never committed. It holds pytest, playwright, websocket-client, and nothing else; every command in this corpus and in CONTRIBUTING.md calls .venv/bin/python (or .venv/bin/python -m pytest, .venv/bin/python -m taf), never the bare python, so a run cannot silently pick up the system's package set. The generated suites it runs are in generated/; the emitters it runs are taf/. Touch it bash cat .venv/pyvenv.cfg which interpreter it shadows, and how it was made .venv/bin/python -m pip list what this project alone has installed python3 -m venv /tmp/other make a second one — the cost is seconds Delete .venv/ entirely and nothing is lost: python3 -m venv .venv plus the README's install line rebuilds it. Neighbors Environment variables are a different thing sharing the word — CONCEPT-python-v1-001-01-the-environment. What the venv installs is pytest and Playwright: CONCEPT-pytest-v1-001-01-the-pytest-fixture, CONCEPT-playwright-v1-002-01-the-browser-context."},{"t":"The learning path","i":"CONCEPT-taf-v1-001-01-the-learning-path","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-taf-v1-001-01-the-learning-path.html","s":"draft","x":"The learning path Definition The ordered index of this corpus's concept documents, junior-first. The corpus is flat and wiki-linked; this page is the sequence a reader new to test automation can follow, each entry naming its prerequisites. Why it exists ADR-taf-v1-002-01 makes the delivered project a learning surface: every concept the generated code embodies is a page here, and generated files cite their concepts with @concept lines the checker enforces. This page answers \"where do I start?\" The path 1. What a test is → CONCEPT-testing-v1-001-01-arrange-act-assert 2. Finding things on a page → CONCEPT-playwright-v1-001-01-the-locator-ladder 3. Knowing where you are → the state marker (SPEC-taf-v1-001-04, states) 4. The model → CONCEPT-dsl-v1-001-01-the-sut-model 5. From model to suite → the emitter and the manifest (SPEC-taf-v1-001-04) 6. Your own toolchain → CONCEPT-python-v1-002-01-the-virtual-environment 7. pytest mechanics → CONCEPT-pytest-v1-001-01-the-pytest-fixture 8. Playwright mechanics → CONCEPT-playwright-v1-002-01-the-browser-context, CONCEPT-playwright-v1-003-01-auto-waiting 9. Organizing a suite → CONCEPT-pom-v1-001-01-the-page-object 10. Configuring a run → CONCEPT-python-v1-001-01-the-environment 11. Closing the loop → CONCEPT-taf-v1-002-01-the-result-sink 12. Java parity — what \"equivalent suite\" means → CONCEPT-java-v1-001-01-the-junit-lifecycle 13. The graph — walks, seeds, coverage → CONCEPT-dsl-v1-002-01-the-seeded-walk Every entry is a checked citation — the corpus checker refuses a link to a page that does not exist."},{"t":"The result sink","i":"CONCEPT-taf-v1-002-01-the-result-sink","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-taf-v1-002-01-the-result-sink.html","s":"draft","x":"The result sink Definition Where a finished test run sends what it found. A sink is a side channel: the suite never waits on it, and a sink that cannot file warns instead of failing the suite a second time. The first sink is the IKC3 tracker — a red test dispatches :support/file-report over the bus's WebSocket, and the tracker writes a :fault-report frame with its own page. Because the manifest maps each test to its journey, the report names the model element that failed, not only the test. python rid = ikc3sink.filereport( \"ws://127.0.0.1:4856/bus-ws\", \"taf journey failed: boardtotot — TimeoutError …\", url=page.url, dom=page.content()[:50000]) Why it exists A suite that only turns red ends the loop in a log file. The sink closes it: fail → report → ticket → fix → repaired is the quality loop the whole platform demonstrates, and the tracker is both the SUT and the tracker. In this repo taf/sinkikc3.py — the wire protocol and filereport. Emitted verbatim into every Python suite (ikc3sink.py) and driven by the pytestruntestmakereport hook in conftest.py when TAFIKC3WS is set. Verified live in tests/testlive.py (opt-in TAFLIVE=1)."},{"t":"Arrange–Act–Assert","i":"CONCEPT-testing-v1-001-01-arrange-act-assert","h":"doc/concepts/CONCEPT-testing-v1-001-01-arrange-act-assert.html","s":"draft","x":"Arrange–Act–Assert Definition The shape of a readable test: arrange the preconditions, act once, assert the outcome. Three phases, in that order, visible in the code. python def testticketmovestotriage(boardpage): ticket = boardpage.newticket(\"the report\") arrange ticket.moveto(\"triage\") act expect(boardpage.ticket(\"the report\")).tobein(\"triage\") assert Why it exists A test is documentation that runs. When the three phases are separated, a reader sees what is tested before how; when they interleave, the intent drowns in mechanics. In tafbase the phases come from the model: a journey's setup is its start state, the act is its steps, and the assertions are the state markers and expect-final the emitter writes for every arrival — so generated tests are AAA by construction. In this repo Generated suites under tools/ (lane B-2). Emitted tests carry @concept CONCEPT-testing-v1-001-01-arrange-act-assert."},{"t":"ADR-taf-v1-001-01 — tafbase stands alone","i":"ADR-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase-stands-alone","h":"doc/decisions/ADR-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase-stands-alone.html","s":"accepted","x":"ADR-taf-v1-001-01 — tafbase stands alone Decided: 2026-10-14 · George Petrov + Devin Decisions 1. tafbase is its own repository and its own corpus. It does not live inside IKC3. gaks.project.json declares it; the static GAKS bundle of PLAN-ikc-v3-079-01 delivers the corpus tools. This is the first real consumer of the foreign-project path. 2. The DSL is JSON, not EDN. The SUT model must be readable and writable from Java and Python with no Clojure dependency — the same reason gaks.project.json replaced the EDN declaration in IKC3. 3. Generate, don't interpret. Emitters write committed, reviewable test files (testlogin.py, LoginJourneyTest.java). A generic runtime interpreter was considered and refused: generated code diffs in a PR are legible; hidden magic is not. 4. Tracker integrations are declared adapters, not a hard dependency. tafbase core runs with no IKC3. The IKC3 adapter (fault reports, repaired-on-merge) is the first sink and the demo loop; JIRA and GitHub Issues adapters are additions of the same kind. 5. The IKC3 tracker is the first SUT, not a component of tafbase. Dogfooding means tests exercise the real platform and file reports into it — not that tafbase embeds it. 6. MIT license. The goals are exposure and adoption; copyleft and dual-licensing are refused. Consequences Doc ids take the taf subproject prefix (PLAN-taf-v1-NNN-NN); numbering is this repository's own. The IKC3 deployment tracker can file tickets for tafbase work only if this corpus is registered as a vault of the deployment — an open question, deferred to the first lane."},{"t":"ADR-taf-v1-002-01 — the delivered project is a learning surface","i":"ADR-taf-v1-002-01-the-learning-surface","h":"doc/decisions/ADR-taf-v1-002-01-the-learning-surface.html","s":"accepted","x":"ADR-taf-v1-002-01 — the delivered project is a learning surface Decided: 2026-10-14 · George Petrov + Devin Context The precedent is Python-Selenium-Chrome-Demo/v.0.1/docs/concepts.md: a hand-written glossary of 60 concepts — Python, Selenium, pytest, design patterns, testing — each with a definition, an example, and a \"Used in\" pointer. It was good teaching material and it could rot: the pointers were prose, checked by nobody. Decisions 7. The corpus teaches. CONCEPT documents in arch-root/concepts/ cover the implicit and explicit concepts the project embodies, across four domains: the language and runner (Python: fixtures, yield, dataclasses; pytest/JUnit: discovery, markers, parametrize), the tool (Playwright/Selenium: locators, auto-waiting, browser contexts), test design (AAA, page object, isolation, flaky tests), and the DSL itself (model, emitter, manifest, parity, refusal). A concept is a page — a definition, a small example, where it lives in this repo — never a reference manual. 8. Citations are checked, not promised. Generated code carries @concept CONCEPT-taf-v1-NNN-NN- comment lines, the form the GAKS code.annotation-pattern already checks (code-link-unresolved fails when a cited concept has no document). The emitter decides which concepts each generated file demonstrates and writes the annotations; the manifest records the concept ids per test. An annotation with no document is a defect the checker refuses. A document nothing cites is not: a concept may be written before the code that embodies it — the learning path marks such pages as waiting on their lane, and a corpus report may list them, but the check does not fail on them. 9. A guided path orders the glossary. The corpus is flat and wiki-linked, but a learner needs a sequence. One concept document — CONCEPT-taf-v1-001-01-the-learning-path — is the ordered index: what a test is → AAA → locators → fixtures → the model → the emitters. Junior-first; each entry names its prerequisites. 10. PRINCIPLE documents join the corpus. tafbase's gaks.project.json declares the PRINCIPLE kind so the platform's own rules (DSL First, release-note-first, one-form-only) are corpus documents that code and plans can cite, not folklore in a README. Consequences Every emitter lane ships the concept documents its generated code cites; the corpus check is the gate — a lane that emits an uncited concept does not pass. The learning surface is a property of the delivered bundle, not of ikc3: gaks/site/ renders the concepts and their backlinks with no server, on tafbase.com or off a disk."},{"t":"PLAN-taf-v1-001-01 — tafbase: a DSL First test automation framework","i":"PLAN-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase","h":"doc/plans/PLAN-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase.html","s":"draft","x":"PLAN-taf-v1-001-01 — tafbase: a DSL First test automation framework Status: in progress tafbase declares a system under test (SUT) once, as data, and generates real test suites from the declaration. Python+Playwright is the first emitter; Java is the second, and the second exists to make the parity claim true. Generated suites report into pluggable tracker adapters; the first adapter is IKC3's fault reports, so a red test files a bug into the very platform the tests exercise. Release note (draft) New: a SUT declared once in JSON runs as a Python+Playwright suite; the same declaration refuses the same broken model the same way. Accepted by: T-taf-001 (parity of refusal, env -i, python3 only) New: a failed generated test files a fault report into a configured tracker through a declared adapter; the IKC3 adapter is the first. Accepted by: T-taf-002 Internal: the Java+Playwright emitter proves the same declaration generates equivalent suites in two languages. Accepted by: T-taf-003 (Python and Java emitters agree on the suite) What this does not do v1 generates static journeys only. Model-based generation — walks of the SUT graph, coverage-directed paths, journal replay — is declared in ADR-taf-v1-001-01 and arrives by lanes, not all at once. Lanes ▶ Lane TAF/PLAN-taf-v1-001-01/A-1 — the SUT model is JSON The contract: SPEC-taf-v1-001-04-tafbase. A SUT declares states, transitions and journeys in one JSON document. The loader validates shape, refusals name the broken clause, and no ambiguous form is guessed — one form only. Release note (draft) New: sut.model.json declares pages, actions and journeys; a broken model names its fault. Accepted by: T-taf-001 ▶ Lane TAF/PLAN-taf-v1-001-01/B-2 — the Python+Playwright emitter From a valid model, taf emit --lang python writes a runnable Playwright+pytest suite: page objects, journey tests, and a manifest mapping each test back to the model element that produced it. Release note (draft) New: one declaration produces a committed, reviewable pytest suite. Accepted by: T-taf-004 ▶ Lane TAF/PLAN-taf-v1-001-01/C-3 — tracker adapters and fault reports Progress (2026-10-15). The IKC3 sink exists and works live: taf/sinkikc3.py dispatches :support/file-report over /bus-ws (EDN envelope, ikc3-bus subprotocol, no token needed where authz is off). Emitted verbatim into each Python suite as ikc3sink.py; the generated pytestruntestmakereport hook files on any red test when TAFIKC3WS is set, carrying page URL + DOM. Proven against a probe bus: a suite pointed at a dead SUT filed one report per red test (tests/testlive.py, opt-in). Still open: the Java-side hook, screenshot/attachment upload, auth-token support, a second (JIRA-shaped) adapter. A test outcome flows to a declared sink. The IKC3 adapter files a fault report and links it to the test's model element; adapters are a declared kind so JIRA or GitHub Issues are additions, not rewrites. Release note (draft) New: a red test files a fault report into IKC3, named for i"},{"t":"The interview surface","i":"RESEARCH-taf-v1-001-01-the-interview-surface","h":"doc/research/RESEARCH-taf-v1-001-01-the-interview-surface.html","s":"draft","x":"The interview surface What this is A coverage map: the corpus's concepts against the questions the market actually asks. Source: a widely-shared \"Top 25 Playwright interview questions\" list (LinkedIn, 2026 SDET interview prep). The corpus's stated job is to serve as a learning surface and interview prep — this document measures that promise against an external yardstick and names the gaps as work. The map interview asks covered by state --- --- --- --- 1 Playwright vs Selenium — gap 2 Project setup (TypeScript assumed) — gap 3 Browser / BrowserContext / Page CONCEPT-playwright-v1-002-01-the-browser-context part 4 Auto-waiting, actionability CONCEPT-playwright-v1-003-01-auto-waiting covered 5 locator() vs page.$() CONCEPT-playwright-v1-001-01-the-locator-ladder part — locator-vs-$ deserves a line 6 Dynamic elements, flaky tests — gap 7 getByRole vs getByText vs css/xpath CONCEPT-playwright-v1-001-01-the-locator-ladder covered 8 Dropdowns, checkboxes, alerts, iframes — gap 9 File upload/download — gap 10 Screenshots/videos on failure CONCEPT-taf-v1-002-01-the-result-sink (our answer is richer: DOM + report) part 11 API testing via request context the model's api/save/expect steps covered 12 Authentication reuse — gap 13 storageState — gap 14 Parallel execution, workers — gap 15 Sharding — gap 16 Retries, flaky-test investigation — gap 17 beforeEach/afterEach CONCEPT-java-v1-001-01-the-junit-lifecycle, CONCEPT-pytest-v1-001-01-the-pytest-fixture covered (in two idioms) 18 Framework architecture: POM + fixtures CONCEPT-pom-v1-001-01-the-page-object + the whole generated layout covered — our strongest answer 19 HTML reports the manifest + sink part — ours is better (traceable), say so 20 CI/CD integration — gap 21 Cross-browser (chromium/firefox/webkit) — gap — model has no browser clause yet 22 API mocking, network interception — gap 23 Debugging: trace viewer, headed, inspector — gap — not asked but we answer it seeded walks, parity contracts, checked citations, refusals our differentiators What the map says Seven covered outright, 4 partial, 14 gaps. The gaps cluster into themes, each of which is a lane, not just a page: Flakiness & resilience (6, 16): retry policy, quarantine — a real feature lane, not only docs. Element dynamics (8, 9): dropdowns/iframes/uploads — the step language grows (select exists; frame, upload, dialog don't). Auth & state reuse (12, 13): storageState maps cleanly to a model clause — declared once, applied to journeys that need it. Execution control (14, 15, 20, 21): workers, shards, CI matrix, browsers — belongs in a run clause or per-suite config. Observability (10, 19, 22, 23): screenshots on failure, Playwright trace, API mocking — lanes of their own. The TypeScript question The market's assumed Playwright language is TypeScript (the question list presumes it). A third emitter is the right move — and cheap: taf/ops.py already carries the canonical plan, so emitts.py renders syntax only, and the parity tests generalize t"},{"t":"The harvest from qa-tafbase","i":"RESEARCH-taf-v1-002-01-the-harvest-from-qa-tafbase","h":"doc/research/RESEARCH-taf-v1-002-01-the-harvest-from-qa-tafbase.html","s":"draft","x":"The harvest from qa-tafbase What this is A previous-generation framework — a Java/Selenium/Cucumber suite (testsbase engine + per-product modules, JUnit4, Appium) — was mapped end-to-end for reusable design. This document keeps the result and the decisions it produced. The raw harvest: 26 ideas with evidence paths. The rules the harvest confirmed Declared beats interpreted. What the old runtime computed — device capability strings, tag→env mapping, layered properties — tafbase emits at codegen. The generator absorbs what a factory once computed. Dwell is for observation, not synchronization. visibilitySleep (a global + per-call-site Thread.sleep around every action) answered two needs at once: letting JS settle and letting a human watch. Auto-waiting markers own the first; a declared dwell owns the second. They must not fuse — sleeps-for-correctness is the disease this framework is built to avoid. Adopted (lanes in PLAN-taf-v1-001-01) idea becomes lane --- --- --- visibilitySleep pacing dwell — per-step/ms or a suite profile, emitted as a literal H-8 named wait vocabulary + soft/hard wait/check step forms, wait any (X Y) H-8 version × language locator matrix versioned elements, generated lookup tables I-9 screenshot grammar + approval loop compare screen step, TCIDrunresstep{E,A,D}, taf promote J-10 capability token (Pixel9ProXLchrome) device:/env: model header emitting the driver block K-11 column-model schema (its TSV interpreter) stays as proof the grammar works — the tafbase DSL is its compile-time heir (settled) rerun.txt failed-only replay taf run --last-failed manifest L-12 debug affordances --debug: keep browser open, break on failure L-12 circuit breaker / preflight requires: model clause → emitted preflight L-12 self-narrating actions required human label on elements/steps → emitted logs H-8 the never-built .srt subtitles narrated steps + timestamps → sidecar .srt beside run video H-8 (with J-10 artifacts) The .srt item deserves a note: all five teardown paths of the old framework captured a video-stop timestamp and called a createSubTitlesFile that was only ever committed commented out — the intent was a subtitle track mapping each narrated action line (Clicking 'X' on 'Y') onto video timecodes. The hard half — timestamped narration — already existed. In generated code both halves are cheap: emitted steps know their text and their clock, so a teardown writer emits results/ / .srt beside the video. A run becomes replayable and readable. Deliberately left behind PPTX reports, Robot/AWT screen capture, hotkey-driven video, Swing overlay strips, VM-awake mouse jiggles, X:-drive archiving, hardcoded notification tokens. Playwright viewport capture + HTML reports + committed artifacts replace the whole cluster."},{"t":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-01 — the SUT model","i":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-01-the-sut-model","h":"doc/specs/SPEC-taf-v1-001-01-the-sut-model.html","s":"superseded","x":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-01 — the SUT model Superseded by SPEC-taf-v1-001-02-the-sut-model — same contract, rewritten normative with numbered clauses; kept as history. Related: PLAN-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase ADR-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase-stands-alone The design input of lane TAF/PLAN-taf-v1-001-01/A-1. This document is the contract: a loader and an emitter are correct together when each refusal and each generated behavior below holds. Purpose A system under test (SUT) is declared once, as one JSON document. The declaration is the model. From it, emitters write runnable suites (Python+Playwright, later Java+Playwright) that follow Playwright's own rules: semantic locators, auto-waiting assertions, one journey per test. The same document is the graph the MBT lanes walk later — states and transitions are first-class from v1, so no model is written twice. The document One file per SUT, sut.model.json by convention. Top level: json { \"sut\": {\"name\": \"ikc3-tracker\", \"base-url\": \"http://127.0.0.1:4855\"}, \"states\": {}, \"elements\": {}, \"actions\": {}, \"journeys\": {}, \"endpoints\": {} } sut name (string, required) names the SUT in refusals and the manifest. base-url (string, required) is where journeys begin. No environment indirection in v1: a different deployment is a different file, or a later lane adds env. states A state is a place a journey can be — a page, a dialog, a verified condition. A state is recognized, not assumed: json \"states\": { \"board\": { \"route\": \"/index.html?island=board\", \"marker\": {\"role\": \"heading\", \"name\": \"Board\"} } } route (optional) is the path under base-url that reaches the state directly. marker (required) is a locator that a generated test asserts visible after every arrival — a state is entered only when the SUT proves it. That one rule is what makes generated tests self-verifying instead of hopeful. elements Named locators, declared once, reused everywhere: json \"elements\": { \"new-ticket-button\": {\"role\": \"button\", \"name\": \"New ticket\"}, \"title-field\": {\"label\": \"Title\"}, \"report-card\": {\"testid\": \"report-card\"}, \"legacy-panel\": {\"css\": \".panel--legacy\"} } THE LOCATOR LADDER is the \"proper use\" clause, encoded, not documented: 1. role + name — the accessibility query, preferred 2. label / text / placeholder — user-visible queries 3. testid — the deliberate escape (data-testid) 4. css / xpath — the last resort, allowed and flagged fragile in the manifest A locator with no usable form is refused :bad-locator. The ladder is the emitter's only ordering; a model does not say which rung a locator sits on, the shape does. actions An action is a step the SUT offers — the transitions of the graph: json \"actions\": { \"move-to-triage\": { \"from\": \"backlog\", \"to\": \"triage\", \"do\": [ {\"click\": \"new-ticket-button\"}, {\"fill\": {\"element\": \"title-field\", \"value\": \"the report\"}}, {\"click\": \"save-button\"}, {\"api\": \"move-ticket\", \"args\": {\"category\": \"triage\"}} ] } } from and to name states and are optional per action, required for the graph to be a graph: an acti"},{"t":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-02 — the SUT model","i":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-02-the-sut-model","h":"doc/specs/SPEC-taf-v1-001-02-the-sut-model.html","s":"superseded","x":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-02 — the SUT model Superseded by SPEC-taf-v1-001-03-the-sut-language — the same contract reframed as a language specification: the JSON is the concrete syntax of the DSL, not the subject. Kept as history. Purpose tafbase is a test automation framework: a web application is described once, as one JSON document, and that document is compiled into runnable browser and API test suites — Playwright for Python and Java today. This document specifies that JSON document. It is the contract between a model author and the emitters: the implementation is correct when all clauses in this document hold. Read this document on its own — it uses no other document of this project. Keywords must, must not, should, and may are normative, in the RFC 2119 sense. Clauses carry identifiers (SM-n) so a test, a review, or a refusal can name what it verifies. Related: PLAN-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase ADR-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase-stands-alone ADR-taf-v1-002-01-the-learning-surface Terms term meaning --- --- SUT the system under test — the application the generated suite exercises model one JSON document declaring a SUT's states, elements, actions, journeys, endpoints state a recognizable place in the SUT — a page, a dialog — proven by its marker marker a locator a generated test asserts visible on arrival at a state locator one form of finding an element — role+name, label, text, placeholder, testid, css, xpath action a named step the SUT offers; with from/to it is an edge of the graph journey a named path through the graph; emitted as one test refusal a named rejection {clause, reason, message}; a broken model emits nothing emitter a code generator: a model in, a runnable suite out manifest taf.manifest.json — emitted beside the tests, mapping each test to its model elements SM-1 · One file, JSON A SUT is declared in one file, .sut.model.json by convention. It must be a JSON object; a document that is not is refused :not-json. A name declared twice in one clause is refused :duplicate-name — JSON's silent last-write-wins is not accepted. Top level: json { \"sut\": {\"name\": \"ikc3-tracker\", \"base-url\": \"http://127.0.0.1:4856\"}, \"states\": {}, \"elements\": {}, \"actions\": {}, \"journeys\": {}, \"endpoints\": {} } sut.name and sut.base-url are required (:missing-field). A model with no states and no journeys is refused :empty-model. elements, actions, and endpoints may be absent or empty. SM-2 · States are recognized, not assumed json \"states\": { \"board\": {\"route\": \"/index.html?island=board\", \"marker\": {\"role\": \"heading\", \"name\": \"Board\"}} } A state must declare marker, a locator (SM-3); a generated test must assert it visible on every arrival — the SUT proves the state, the test does not assume it (:missing-field without one). route is optional; when present it is the path under base-url that reaches the state directly. A state no journey can reach is refused :unreachable — a declared state with no path to it is a dead declaration. SM-3 · The locator ladder A locator must take "},{"t":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-03 — the SUT language","i":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-03-the-sut-language","h":"doc/specs/SPEC-taf-v1-001-03-the-sut-language.html","s":"superseded","x":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-03 — the SUT language Superseded by SPEC-taf-v1-001-04-tafbase — the subject is the framework, not the language; the DSL is its interface, usable without knowing it is one. Kept as history. Purpose tafbase is a DSL First test automation framework. Its input is a small language for declaring a system under test (SUT): the states a user can reach, the actions that move between them, and the journeys a suite must prove. From one declaration, emitters compile runnable browser and API test suites — Playwright for Python and Java today. This document specifies that language. Part 1 gives the language's constructs and their meaning — the abstract syntax. Part 2 gives the JSON form a model author writes — the concrete syntax. Part 3 gives the contract every implementation must honor: loading, refusals, emission, parity. This document stands alone. Keywords must, must not, should, and may are normative in the RFC 2119 sense. Clauses carry identifiers (SL-n) so a test, a review, or a refusal names what it verifies. Related: PLAN-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase ADR-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase-stands-alone ADR-taf-v1-002-01-the-learning-surface Terms term meaning --- --- DSL domain-specific language — here, the language this document defines SUT the system under test — the application a generated suite exercises model a program in the SUT language: one declaration of a SUT state a recognizable place in the SUT, proven by its marker marker a locator a generated test asserts visible on arrival locator one way to find an element — role+name, label, text, placeholder, testid, css, xpath action a named step the SUT offers; with from/to it is an edge journey a named path through the graph; compiled to one test refusal a named rejection {clause, reason, message}; a rejected model emits nothing emitter a compiler backend: a model in, a runnable suite out manifest taf.manifest.json — maps each emitted test to its model elements Part 1 — The constructs SL-1 · States are recognized, not assumed A state is where a journey can be. It declares a marker — the proof — and may declare a route, the address that reaches it directly. A test arrives at a state only when the SUT shows the marker. This is the language's core semantics: a state is proven, never presumed. SL-2 · Locators form a ladder A locator takes exactly one form, and the forms are ordered by what a user perceives: rung form standing --- --- --- 1 role + name how a user names a control — preferred 2 label / text / placeholder what a user reads 3 testid a deliberate testing hook 4 css / xpath structure, fragile — flagged in the manifest The ladder is a language rule, not a style guide: the shape of a locator chooses its rung, and rung 4 carries a fragile mark into the manifest. SL-3 · Actions are edges An action is a step the SUT offers. An action that declares from and to is an edge of the SUT's graph — the same graph the MBT lanes walk (taf walk), so a model is written once and serves journeys, walks, and cover"},{"t":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-04 — tafbase","i":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-04-tafbase","h":"doc/specs/SPEC-taf-v1-001-04-tafbase.html","s":"draft","x":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-04 — tafbase Purpose tafbase is a test automation framework. You describe a system under test (SUT) once — its screens, its controls, the journeys that matter — and tafbase generates runnable browser and API test suites: pytest + Playwright for Python, JUnit 5 + Playwright for Java. The description is a small DSL — but using tafbase needs no knowledge of languages or code generation. You fill in a JSON form; the framework turns it into tests you can read, run, and review. Everything in this document is about what you declare and what you get. This document stands alone. Keywords must, must not, should, and may are normative in the RFC 2119 sense. Clauses carry identifiers (TAF-n) so a test, a review, or a refusal names what it verifies. Related: PLAN-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase ADR-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase-stands-alone ADR-taf-v1-002-01-the-learning-surface Terms term meaning --- --- SUT the system under test — the application a generated suite exercises model your declaration of a SUT, written in tafbase's DSL state a recognizable place in the SUT, proven by its marker marker a locator a generated test asserts visible on arrival locator one way to find an element — role+name, label, text, placeholder, testid, css, xpath action a named step the SUT offers; with from/to it is an edge journey a named path through the SUT; compiled to one test refusal a named rejection {clause, reason, message}; a rejected model emits nothing emitter a generator: a model in, a runnable suite out manifest taf.manifest.json — maps each generated test to your model's elements walk a journey the tool derives by traversing the graph, replayable by seed Part 1 — What you declare TAF-1 · States are recognized, not assumed A state is where a journey can be — a page, a dialog, a verified condition. It declares a marker: the visible proof. A test arrives at a state only when the SUT shows that proof. A state may also declare a route, the address that reaches it directly. TAF-2 · Locators form a ladder A locator takes exactly one form. The forms are ordered by what a user perceives, and the order is a rule of the framework, not a style guide: rung form standing --- --- --- 1 role + name how a user names a control — preferred 2 label / text / placeholder what a user reads 3 testid a deliberate testing hook 4 css / xpath structure, fragile — flagged in the manifest The shape of a locator chooses its rung; you never declare one. Named locators are declared once in elements and used by name everywhere. TAF-3 · Actions are edges An action is a step the SUT offers. An action with from and to is an edge of the SUT's graph — which is also what taf walk traverses, so a model serves journeys, walks, and coverage without being written twice. An action's do list composes steps: act (click, fill, check, select), move (navigate), observe (wait-for, expect), call the API (api, save), pace (dwell — a pause for a watching human, never a synchronization device; markers and wait-for own t"}];
